  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7265114aa1e7f41ada19d74a427f9e13, type: 3}
  m_Name: TimelineCutsceneCard
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: xpTURN.Feedback.Samples::xpTURN.Feedback.Samples.SampleCard
  Title: Unity Timeline Cutscene
  Try: 'Watch the six-second cutscene loop, then pause it and drag the slider: the
    camera shake and the hero''s spring follow the timeline, the flash holds. In
    the Editor, select Cutscene and open Window > Sequencing > Timeline to scrub
    it in Edit mode (the burst plays in Play mode only).'
  See: "A Feedback track for each player on one timeline (the Unity Timeline package).
    Driven clips \u2014 the camera shake and the hero's looping spring \u2014 take
    the timeline's time: a scrub, a pause and the director's speed show the same
    values, and the clip's Seed gives the same shake each time. A Triggered clip
    \u2014 the screen flash \u2014 starts with its clip and runs on its player's
    clock. A Signal at 4.5 s calls the burst's PlayFromEvent through a Signal Receiver,
    in Play mode only: the Timeline window's preview sends no signal while scrubbing,
    and the receiver's call is Runtime Only.
    Each clip ends with Revert. The HUD's intensity does not reach the clips (a clip's
    Intensity is its own) and its time scale slows the director with the game. Before
    leaves out the camera and the motion, and starts the cutscene over (a play keeps
    the categories it began with); the flash is out of every category."
  Controls: "Space \xB7 1 \xB7 Gamepad South \u2014 play from the start \xB7 2 \xB7
    Gamepad West \u2014 pause \xB7 resume \xB7 Z \xB7 X \xB7 D-pad \u2014 scrub \xB7
    Mouse \u2014 buttons \xB7 slider"
  SceneGuid: cc5f520cc434f4f039c2c9ae46695dbf
  SceneName: TimelineCutscene
  RequiredType: xpTURN.Feedback.Samples.Advanced.TimelineCutsceneLab, xpTURN.Feedback.Samples.Advanced.TimelineCutscene
  RequiresText: Timeline 1.8 or later
  OpenInEditor: 1
